  "headline": "Oil is repricing rates, not growth: yields at 52-week highs while copper and bitcoin hold",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as an energy-led inflation shock passing straight into the rates market. Brent jumped 8.5% today and is up 20% over the month to near $106; the 10-year yield has climbed 52 basis points over the same month to 5.16%, a 52-week high, with the 30-year at 5.46%. The 30y–10y spread has flattened 24 basis points in a month — the shorter leg rising faster — which is the bond market pricing tighter policy to meet inflation, not stronger long-run growth.",
    "Gold is the tell that this is a real-yield story rather than a fear trade: down 8% over the month even as oil surges and the dollar index gains 2.4%. Growth signals are intact — copper sits at 94% of its 52-week range, up 20% year to date, the copper/gold ratio is up 10% on the month, and bitcoin, up 11% this week and 39% over three months, buys 17% more gold than a month ago. Risk appetite has not cracked.",
    "The tensions: bitcoin rallying into 5%-plus long yields, and USD/CAD up 1.9% on the month despite the oil spike, meaning dollar strength is overriding commodity-currency logic. The rates-and-dollar leg carries more weight; it is consistent across the curve, gold and FX. Stagflationary in prices, not yet in activity."
